18. ADMIN AND INTERVIEWER SETUP GUIDE

This section explains how to enable OAuth sign-in, create the first administrator,
assign roles, and use the interviewer review dashboard.


18.1 REQUIRED SOFTWARE

- Python 3.8 or later.
- A virtual environment in the project folder.
- Google OAuth credentials, GitHub OAuth credentials, or both.
- Internet access while using Google/GitHub sign-in.


18.2 INSTALL DEPENDENCIES

Open PowerShell in the project folder and run:

    cd D:\AI-Interview
    .\venv\Scripts\python.exe -m pip install -r requirements.txt

The Authlib package in requirements.txt enables Google and GitHub OAuth.


18.3 CREATE THE ENVIRONMENT FILE

The application reads credentials from a file named .env in the same folder as
app.py:

    D:\AI-Interview\.env

The .env.example file is only a template. Values placed only in .env.example are
not loaded by the application.

Use this format in .env. Do not add spaces around the equals sign.

    SECRET_KEY=replace-with-a-long-random-secret
    GEMINI_API_KEY=optional-gemini-key
    GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID=your-google-client-id
    GOOGLE_CLIENT_SECRET=your-google-client-secret
    GITHUB_CLIENT_ID=your-github-client-id
    GITHUB_CLIENT_SECRET=your-github-client-secret
    ADMIN_EMAILS=your-email@example.com
    SESSION_COOKIE_SECURE=false

Use a long random value for SECRET_KEY. Never upload .env to GitHub, because it
contains secrets.


18.4 CONFIGURE GOOGLE OAUTH

1. Open Google Cloud Console.
2. Create or select a Google Cloud project.
3. Configure the OAuth consent screen.
4. Create an OAuth Client ID with application type: Web application.
5. Add this authorized redirect URI for local development:

    http://127.0.0.1:5000/auth/google/callback

6. Copy the Google Client ID and Client Secret to GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID and
   GOOGLE_CLIENT_SECRET in .env.

Do not choose the Desktop application type for this Flask web application.


18.5 CONFIGURE GITHUB OAUTH

1. Open GitHub Developer Settings.
2. Create a new OAuth App.
3. Use this homepage URL for local development:

    http://127.0.0.1:5000

4. Use this authorization callback URL:

    http://127.0.0.1:5000/auth/github/callback

5. Generate a client secret and copy its Client ID and Client Secret to
   GITHUB_CLIENT_ID and GITHUB_CLIENT_SECRET in .env.


18.6 START THE APPLICATION

Run:

    cd D:\AI-Interview
    .\venv\Scripts\python.exe app.py

Then open:

    http://127.0.0.1:5000

If client ID and client secret values are present in .env, the Sign in page shows
the configured Google and/or GitHub sign-in buttons.


18.7 VERIFY OAUTH CONFIGURATION WITHOUT REVEALING SECRETS

Open a second PowerShell window in D:\AI-Interview and run:

    .\venv\Scripts\python.exe -c "from config import Config; print('Google ID:', bool(Config.GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID)); print('Google secret:', bool(Config.GOOGLE_CLIENT_SECRET)); print('GitHub ID:', bool(Config.GITHUB_CLIENT_ID)); print('GitHub secret:', bool(Config.GITHUB_CLIENT_SECRET))"

All configured providers should show True for both their ID and secret.


18.8 ROLES

Candidate
    Can sign in, upload a resume, configure an interview, answer questions, and
    view their own current-session feedback.

Interviewer
    Can open the Interviewer Dashboard and review recorded interview sessions,
    question text, submitted answers, durations, score breakdowns, and AI feedback.
    An interviewer cannot assign roles.

Admin
    Has all interviewer permissions and can open User Administration to assign
    candidate, interviewer, and admin roles. An admin cannot change their own role
    from the web interface, which reduces the risk of accidentally removing all
    administrator access.


18.9 CREATE THE FIRST ADMINISTRATOR

1. Put the email address you will use with Google or GitHub in ADMIN_EMAILS.
   Multiple addresses may be separated with commas.
2. Restart the Flask application.
3. Sign in using that same verified provider email.
4. The account is created with the admin role.
5. The Admin navigation link appears after sign-in.

Example:

    ADMIN_EMAILS=admin@example.com,second-admin@example.com


18.10 ASSIGN AN INTERVIEWER OR ADMIN ROLE

1. Ask the user to sign in once. This creates their account in the database.
2. Sign in using an administrator account.
3. In the navigation bar, select Admin.
4. Find the user in User Administration.
5. Select Candidate, Interviewer, or Admin from the role list.
6. Click Save.
7. Ask the user to sign out and sign in again so their menu and permissions refresh.


18.11 REVIEW INTERVIEWS AS AN INTERVIEWER

1. Sign in using an account whose role is Interviewer or Admin.
2. Select Interviewer in the navigation bar.
3. The dashboard lists recorded sessions, candidates, domains, answer counts, and
   average Grammar, Relevance, Confidence, and STAR scores.
4. Select Review for a session.
5. Review the question, candidate answer, answer duration, score breakdown, and
   AI feedback for every submitted answer.

The dashboard shows coaching data. It must not be used as the sole basis for an
employment or academic decision.


18.12 TROUBLESHOOTING

Sign-in buttons do not appear
    Check that credential values are in D:\AI-Interview\.env, not only in
    .env.example. Restart Flask after editing .env.

Google reports redirect URI mismatch
    The Google Cloud OAuth client must be Web application type and must contain
    exactly: http://127.0.0.1:5000/auth/google/callback

GitHub reports redirect URI mismatch
    The GitHub OAuth App callback URL must be exactly:
    http://127.0.0.1:5000/auth/github/callback

User cannot see Interviewer or Admin in the navigation
    An administrator must assign the relevant role. The user must then sign out
    and sign in again.

No users appear in User Administration
    A user appears only after completing their first OAuth sign-in.


18.13 PRODUCTION DEPLOYMENT NOTES

Before deployment:

- Use a production WSGI server instead of Flask's debug server.
- Serve the application through HTTPS.
- Set SESSION_COOKIE_SECURE=true.
- Replace local callback URLs with the exact HTTPS URLs of the deployed app in
  both Google and GitHub OAuth settings.
- Use a strong, unique SECRET_KEY stored in deployment secrets.
- Keep .env outside source control.
- Review data retention and privacy requirements before allowing interviewer access
  to candidate answers.
